To include `document_annotation_format` in a batch OCR request file, add it inside the `body` object of each request entry, alongside other fields like `document`, `bbox_annotation_format`, and `pages`. For example:

```json
{
    "custom_id": "1",
    "body": {
        "document": {"document_url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mistralai/cookbook/refs/heads/main/mistral/ocr/receipt.png"},
        "document_annotation_format": {
            "type": "json_schema",
            "json_schema": {
                "schema": {
                    "properties": {
                        "language": {"title": "Language","type": "string"},
                        "urls": {"title": "urls","type": "string"}
                    },
                    "required": ["language","urls"],
                    "title": "DocumentAnnotation",
                    "type": "object",
                    "additionalProperties": false
                },
                "name": "document_annotation",
                "strict": true
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The batch file follows the same request body format as the OCR endpoint, except the model ID is omitted and provided later during job creation [2].

Yes, you can use the `html` table format to avoid pipe characters. Set the `table_format` parameter to `"html"` in your request. For example:

```typescript
const ocrResponse = await client.ocr.process({
    model: "mistral-ocr-latest",
    document: {
        type: "document_url",
        documentUrl: "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.04234"
    },
    tableFormat: "html", // default is null
    includeImageBase64: true
});
```

This returns tables as HTML, which does not use pipe delimiters [1][2].

